## 6. Application Components ("MiniShop")

### 6a. App Provenance — what's kept / stripped / added from `kuma-demo`

| Item | From upstream `kuma-demo-aio.yaml` | What we do |
|---|---|---|
| Frontend image | `kvn0218/kuma-demo-fe:latest`, port 8080, arg `-P <backend-url>` | **Keep image**, pin by digest, point `-P` arg at plain-DNS backend Service |
| Backend image | `kvn0218/kuma-demo-be:latest`, port 3001 | **Keep image**, pin by digest. **Drop the v1/v2 canary Deployments** (Kuma traffic-routing demo, irrelevant here) — keep only one Deployment, scaled to 3 replicas |
| Postgres | `kvn0218/postgres:latest`, `emptyDir` volume (⚠️ not persistent) | **Replace** with CloudNativePG `Cluster` CRD + real PVC via `local-path` StorageClass |
| Redis | `kvn0218/kuma-redis:latest`, no volume | **Keep image**, add a PVC for AOF persistence, deploy as raw StatefulSet (not CNPG-style) |
| Service discovery | Backend/frontend env vars use Kuma-mesh DNS: `postgres_kuma-demo_svc_5432.mesh`, `REDIS_PORT: "80"` (Envoy sidecar intercept trick) | **Rewrite** to plain k8s DNS + real ports: `POSTGRES_HOST=postgres-rw.data.svc.cluster.local`, `POSTGRES_PORT_NUM=5432`, `REDIS_HOST=redis.data.svc.cluster.local`, `REDIS_PORT=6379` |
| Probes | None defined | **Add** liveness/readiness probes (see 6b) |
| Resource limits | Only on Postgres/Redis, not on app pods | **Add** requests/limits on frontend + backend |
| Namespace/mesh injection | `kuma.io/sidecar-injection: enabled` label, Envoy sidecar containers | **Remove entirely** — no service mesh in this project |
| Domain model | "items" (catalog) + "reviews" (ratings), no write-heavy order flow | **Conceptual rename only**: treat items as `products`. For a real `orders` concept, add a small custom endpoint (Section 6c) — this is the one piece of actual new code in the whole project, and it's optional/small. |

**Licensing note:** `kuma-demo` and the broader Kuma project are Apache-2.0. The demo images are published under an individual's Docker Hub namespace (`kvn0218`) rather than the `kumahq` org — for a learning project this is fine, but pin images by digest (not `:latest`) so the environment stays reproducible, and note this as a "not fit for real production" caveat if this project is ever shown to others.

### 6b. Component Table

| Component | Kind | Image | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `web-frontend` | Deployment, 2 replicas | `kvn0218/kuma-demo-fe` (pinned digest) | Serves the marketplace UI, `-P` arg points at backend Service |
| `api-backend` | Deployment, 3 replicas | `kvn0218/kuma-demo-be` (pinned digest) | REST API for items/reviews. **Add** `GET /healthz`, `GET /ready` (checks DB+Redis reachability — verify actual paths once image is inspected; fall back to a TCP probe if the image has no such route) |
| `db-migration-job` | Job | same backend image or a `psql`/`migrate` sidecar image | Ensures schema exists before first backend start |
| `report-cronjob` | CronJob (every 5 min for demo) | small custom script image (e.g. `postgres:alpine` + `psql` + `redis-cli`) | Reads Postgres/Redis, writes a "sales report" log line |
| `postgres` | CNPG `Cluster` CRD (1 primary + 1 replica) | CNPG-managed Postgres image | PVC via `local-path`; scheduled only on `workload-type=database` node via nodeAffinity + toleration |
| `redis-cache` | Manual StatefulSet, 1 replica | `kvn0218/kuma-redis` (pinned digest) | Headless Service, PVC for AOF; same node targeting as Postgres |

**Init container:** `wait-for-postgres` on `api-backend` (netcat/pg_isready loop against `postgres-rw:5432`) before the app container starts.

**Sidecar:** `log-shipper` on `api-backend` — tails a shared `emptyDir` log volume and forwards to stdout (stand-in for Loki/Fluent-bit), demonstrating the shared-volume sidecar pattern.

**Probes:** liveness = process-alive check; readiness = dependency check. Demo: kill DB connectivity → readiness fails (pod pulled from Service endpoints) without a restart, to make the liveness-vs-readiness distinction concrete.

### 6c. Optional stretch: real "orders" endpoint

`kuma-demo`'s backend only has items + reviews. If you want a genuine write-heavy "orders" flow (useful later for HPA load-testing), add a minimal new route to the backend — a POST `/api/orders` that inserts into a new `orders` table via the same Postgres connection the app already has, plus a GET to list them. This is a few dozen lines against an existing Express app, not a new service — worth doing in Phase 3 once the base app is running, not before.

